  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 3,
  "question": {
    "text": "In a simple pendulum of length I the bob is pulled aside from its equilibrium position through an angle $\\theta$ and then released. The bob passes through the equilibrium position with speed",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "1Jx9V",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{2 g l(1+\\cos \\theta)}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "YlVRl",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{2 g l \\sin \\theta}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "aiZmz",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~g} l}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "gM3LN",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~g} l(1-\\cos \\theta)}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "If $l$ is length of pendulum and $\\theta$ be angular amplitude then from principle of conservation of energy\r\n\r\n(Initial total energy) $0+\\mathrm{mgh}=\\frac{1}{2} m v^{2}+0$ (Final total energy) $\\Rightarrow v=\\sqrt{2 g h}=\\sqrt{2 g l(1-\\cos \\theta)}$",
    "image": null
  },
